From: YOUR NAME <andrew@orospakr.ca>
Subject: [PATCH] Input: synaptics-rmi4 - apply configuration after waking the sensor on SMBus resume

rmi_smb_resume() re-applies the function configuration - rmi_reset() ->
rmi_driver_reset_handler() -> rmi_driver_process_config_requests() -
before rmi_driver_resume(), i.e. while the sensor is still in the sleep
mode that rmi_f01_suspend() selected.  F01's config hook even rewrites
ctrl0 with the sleep bits still set, and F12's control registers are
written to a sleeping sensor.  Only afterwards does rmi_f01_resume()
switch it back to normal mode, and nothing re-applies the configuration
after that.  The i2c and spi transports do not have this problem: they
do not need to reconfigure on resume, since no PS/2 reset happened.

On a Synaptics TM3471 (LEN2073, ThinkPad T14 Gen 2a AMD, RMI4 over
SMBus) this leaves the sensor half-configured after roughly every second
system resume: it keeps reporting, but on a 12.5 ms (80 Hz) clock with
about a quarter of the frames merged into 23-24 ms, instead of the flat
15 ms (~67 Hz) it runs at after probe.  It feels like a slightly mushy
pad; an evtest SYN_REPORT interval histogram shows it clearly.  The
configuration registers read back identically in both states.

Re-running the configuration pass on the awake sensor cures it every
time - at any point from 1 s to 60 s after resume (0 of 29 cycles
degraded), and from rmi_smb_resume() itself right after
rmi_driver_resume() (0 of 8), against ~50% for the current order.

Move the rmi_reset() call after rmi_driver_resume() so the configuration
is written to a sensor that has been woken up.  The transport reset
(rmi_smb_reset()) stays first; it has to precede any register access.

Signed-off-by: YOUR NAME <andrew@orospakr.ca>
---
--- a/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c
+++ b/drivers/input/rmi4/rmi_smbus.c
@@ -380,12 +380,19 @@
 
 	rmi_smb_reset(&rmi_smb->xport, 0);
 
-	rmi_reset(rmi_dev);
-
 	ret = rmi_driver_resume(rmi_smb->xport.rmi_dev, true);
 	if (ret)
 		dev_warn(dev, "Failed to resume device: %d\n", ret);
 
+	/*
+	 * The PS/2 side has reset the sensor, so its configuration has to
+	 * be applied again. Do that only now, after rmi_f01_resume() has
+	 * taken the sensor out of sleep mode: configuring it while it is
+	 * still asleep is not reliable, the sensor may come back reporting
+	 * on a slower clock until the configuration is written again.
+	 */
+	rmi_reset(rmi_dev);
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
